---
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import GuideLayout from "../../components/GuideLayout.astro";
import { watershedCodeTheme } from "../../styles/code-theme";
import { excerpt, section } from "../../lib/excerpt";
import boardOpsSource from "../../../../examples/retro_tutorial_lustre/src/board_ops.gleam?raw";
import boardSource from "../../../../examples/retro_tutorial_lustre/src/board.gleam?raw";
import appSource from "../../../../examples/retro_tutorial_lustre/src/retro_tutorial_lustre.gleam?raw";
import boardTestSource from "../../../../examples/retro_tutorial_lustre/test/board_test.gleam?raw";

const voteOps = excerpt(
  boardOpsSource,
  "pub fn upvote(",
  "pub fn downvote(",
  "fn change_votes(",
);
const readVotes = excerpt(boardOpsSource, "fn vote_entries(");
const attach = excerpt(boardSource, "fn card(");
const voteClicks = section(
  appSource,
  "    UpvoteClicked(id) ->",
  "    FocusClicked(id) -> {",
);
const orphanTest = excerpt(
  boardTestSource,
  "pub fn tallies_attach_to_matching_notes_and_orphans_are_ignored_test(",
);

const lossy = `// The version that loses a vote: a plain number in a SharedMap.
// Two clients read 4, both write 5, and the second write is not wrong —
// it is just the last one.
let current = read_total(board, id)
watershed.set(board, id, json.int(current + 1))`;
---

<GuideLayout
  slug="votes"
  description="Step four of the watershed build guide: put vote totals in a TallyMode OR-map so each key is a PN counter, ship signed deltas instead of computed totals, and let concurrent votes sum instead of overwriting each other."
>
  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>The bug is in the read, not the write</h2>
    <p>
      Step 01’s second race: <code>+1</code>, <code>+1</code>, <code>-1</code> on
      one note, settling at <code>+1</code>. It looks like arithmetic because it
      is arithmetic — right up until you implement it the obvious way.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={lossy} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      Nothing there is careless. It reads, adds, writes. But <em>read</em> is the
      problem: by the time the write is sequenced, the value it was computed from
      is stale, and the newer write silently replaces the older one’s work. Two
      votes go in, one total comes out, and no error is raised anywhere — the
      lost vote leaves no trace at all.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ship the <code>+1</code> instead of the <code>5</code>. Addition commutes,
      so the sequencer’s order stops mattering: whichever way the deltas land,
      the total is the same. That is what a tally is.
    </p>
    <div class="g-note">
      <p>
        <strong>Watch it lose, once.</strong> The{" "}
        <a href="/counter-bug">counter bug</a> page runs the losing version and
        the fix against the same race, live. Reading about a dropped write is
        not the same as watching one disappear.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2><code>TallyMode</code>: a counter per key</h2>
    <p>
      You already made this choice back in step 02, when
      <code>ensure_or_map</code> got <code>TallyMode</code> for the
      <code>votes</code> channel. That mode makes every key in the map a
      PN counter: a leaf that accepts signed deltas and merges by summing them,
      never by picking a winner.
    </p>
    <p>
      Which makes the vote API three lines long, and negative deltas fall out for
      free. Down-voting is not a special case — it is a <code>-1</code>.
    </p>
    <span class="g-file">src/board_ops.gleam</span>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={voteOps} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <span class="g-file">src/retro_tutorial_lustre.gleam</span>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={voteClicks} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      Both branches re-snapshot immediately. The delta applies to your local
      replica the moment you click, so the number moves under your finger and
      then stays put when the sequencer confirms it — the local guess and the
      sequenced result agree, because commutative arithmetic can’t disagree with
      itself.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Two channels, one board</h2>
    <p>
      Notes and votes are separate channels, keyed the same way. Reading the
      votes map is the mirror of reading the notes map: every leaf is a
      <code>Tally</code>, and anything else means the channel was created in the
      wrong mode.
    </p>
    <span class="g-file">src/board_ops.gleam</span>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={readVotes} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      The board joins them by note id when it builds each card. A note with no
      tally shows <code>0</code> rather than nothing, so a fresh note and a note
      voted back down to zero look the same — which is what a reader expects.
    </p>
    <span class="g-file">src/board.gleam</span>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={attach} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <div class="g-note">
      <p>
        <strong>Two channels means two clocks.</strong> A vote can be sequenced
        before the note it belongs to has reached your replica. The join is a
        lookup, not an invariant, so the orphan tally simply has nothing to
        attach to yet — and appears the moment the note lands. Design the read
        so that being briefly out of step is boring.
      </p>
    </div>
    <span class="g-file">test/board_test.gleam</span>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={orphanTest} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
      <figcaption class="annot">
        the tally for a note that isn’t there renders nowhere and breaks nothing
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h3>Deepening: what a tally deliberately can’t do</h3>
    <p>
      A tally converges because it refuses to answer questions about
      <em>who</em>. It has no per-voter record, so it can’t enforce one vote per
      person, can’t give you a vote budget, and can’t tell you whether a total of
      <code>2</code> means two people or one person clicking twice.
    </p>
    <p>
      Those are real features and they need a different structure: an{" "}
      <a href="/structures/sets#orset">OR-set</a> of voter ids per note, or the{" "}
      <a href="/structures/coordination">coordination family</a> when the
      question is “who holds this” rather than “how many”. Reach for them when
      you need them, and not before — a tally that fits is worth more than a
      quorum that doesn’t.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-out">
    <span class="annot">You can now</span>
    <p>
      Open two tabs, hammer <code>+1</code> and <code>-1</code> on the same note
      from both, and get an honest total every time — the same
      <code>+1</code> the step-01 race settles on, now on a live server.
    </p>
  </div>
</GuideLayout>
